  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nWe develop a local search strategy to minimize the worst-case overlap integral using constrained optimization. The Erdos minimum overlap problem is tackled by iteratively improving a candidate step function within a fixed interval. Our method combines local search guided by pairwise comparisons and repair mechanisms to maintain integral constraints without derivative-based optimization, leveraging the provided initial solution as a starting point.\n\nThe core idea is to exploit the structure of the overlap integral through greedy optimization. Given the many-to-sparse nature of the objective function, we first extract the shifts that yield the worst-case overlap and then search locally around these shifts. This reduces the effective search space while preserving integral constraints.\n\nThe rationale is threefold:\n1. Non-convex optimization benefits from multiple initial guesses\n2. The problem is separable in integer solution space\n3. Integral constraints can be maintained independently\n\nMathematical specification:\n- Representation: A step function \\( h: [0, 2] \\to [0, 1] \\) represented by a vector \\(\\mathbf{h} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n_{\\text{points}}}\\) with values in [0,1] and \\(\\sum h = n_{\\text{points}}/2\\).\n- Objective function: Minimize \\(C_5(\\mathbf{h}) = \\frac{2}{n_{\\text{points}}} \\times \\max_k \\operatorname{corr}(\\mathbf{h}, \\mathbf{1-h}[k])\\), where correlation uses mode='full'\n- Constraints: \\(0 \\leq h_i \\leq 1\\) and \\(\\sum h_i = n_{\\text{points}}/2\\)\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Input: `seed`, `budget_s`, and initial solution `initial_h_values`\n2. Initialize:\n   - \\(n_{\\text{points}} = \\text{len}(\\text{initial\\_h\\_values})\\)\n   - \\(T = n_{\\text{points}}/2\\)\n   - n_samples = 50 (sample flips) + 1 (current)\n3. Repair function:\n   - Clip values to [0,1]\n   - Adjust integral by proportional scaling\n4. Evaluate current C5 using full correlation\n5. For each iteration:\n   - Keep best candidate (lowest C5)\n   - Find worst shift for current candidate\n   - Generate candidate modifications centered on worst shift\n   - Repair any resulting infeasible candidates\n   - Update solution if improvement found\n6. Termination:\n   - Return best solution within budget\n\nSearch choices and useful variations:\n- Initial population: Provided initial solution\n- Local search: 20% random flip, 80% local repair\n- Focus: One worst shift per iteration\n- Adaptation: 10/flips for positive shifts, 1.5 for negative\n\nComputational budget and robustness:\n- Each candidate: O(n_points^2) correlation (dominant)\n- Batch of 10 candidates: Repeat 100x with budget\n- Total wall time \u2248 5 million candidate-evaluations\n- Fallback: Return initial solution if repair fails\n\nValidation and return contract:\n- Enforce: 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 and sum(h)==T\n- Check: np.allclose(sum(h), T, rtol=1e-5)\n- Output: (h, current_C5, n_points)",
